"240\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\">red granules</text></g><g><text class=\"t wb-name\" x=\"398\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\">basophil</text><text class=\"t wb-share\" x=\"398\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\">under 1%</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"398\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bilobed nucleus</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"398\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dark blue granules</text></g><g><text class=\"t wb-name\" x=\"82\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\">monocyte</text><text class=\"t wb-share\" x=\"82\" y=\"374\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2 to 8%</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"82\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\">horseshoe nucleus</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"82\" y=\"406\" text-anchor=\"middle\">becomes a macrophage</text></g><g><text class=\"t wb-name\" x=\"246\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\">lymphocytes</text><text class=\"t wb-share\" x=\"246\" y=\"374\" text-anchor=\"middle\">30 to 40%</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"246\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\">round nucleus</text><text class=\"t wb-ft\" x=\"246\" y=\"406\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin rim of cytoplasm</text></g><g><text class=\"t wb-sub\" x=\"204\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\">B, T cells</text><text class=\"t wb-sub mut\" x=\"204\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6 to 8 µm</text><text class=\"t wb-sub\" x=\"286\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\">NK cells</text><text class=\"t wb-sub mut\" x=\"286\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9 to 12 µm</text></g><text class=\"t wb-sub mut\" x=\"410\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\">red cell,</text><text class=\"t wb-sub mut\" x=\"410\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\">for scale</text></svg></div></div></figure><div class=\"am-controls\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Mode\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"mode\" data-amv=\"ex\">Explore</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"mode\" data-amv=\"qz\">Quiz me</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"nx\" data-amv=\"1\">Next clue</button></div>\n<div class=\"am-controls\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Cell\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"sel\" data-amv=\"neu\">Neutrophil</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"sel\" data-amv=\"eos\">Eosinophil</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"sel\" data-amv=\"bas\">Basophil</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"sel\" data-amv=\"mono\">Monocyte</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amk=\"sel\" data-amv=\"lym\">Lymphocyte</button></div><p class=\"am-summary\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p><p class=\"am-info\">Hover or tap a part of the drawing to read about it.</p><details class=\"am-sources\"><summary>What this shows, and what's simplified</summary><ul><li>Checked against the recording of the lecture (Tuesday, November 17, 1 pm): he said what you need to remember is two classes and five types, gave each cell's share aloud, and described each one as drawn here (the neutrophil the first line of defense against bacteria, about 10 hours in the blood; the monocyte's single nucleus, not lobed; the NK cells \"pretty large\" and the B and T cells \"a little bit smaller\"). He said there are no stained images on the exam, which is why these are drawings.</li><li>From the L19 notes (Dr. Herr's slides and their speaker notes): the two classes and five types; each cell's share of the white count; the neutrophil's multi-lobed nucleus, glycogen and two types of granules (primary: elastase and myeloperoxidase; secondary: lysozyme and proteases), about 10 hours in the blood; the eosinophil's bilobed nucleus, red-staining granules, peroxidase and major basic protein, parasites, hay fever and IgE receptors; the basophil's bilobed nucleus, dark blue to purple granules, heparin, histamine, IgE receptors and asthma; the monocyte's indented or horseshoe-shaped nucleus, lysosomes, about 3 days in the blood, the macrophage and MHC class II; the lymphocyte's spherical, slightly indented nucleus and thin rim of light-staining cytoplasm, small B and T cells and large NK cells.</li><li>Three slide errors, drawn the way the notes teach them. Slide 6 labels the neutrophil's secondary granules \"Azur granules\", and he read them that way aloud (\"azure granules, which are going to contain lysozyme and proteases\"); histology texts (Junqueira, Ross) call the primary granules azurophilic and the secondary ones specific, so the primary granules are drawn as the darker azurophilic ones and the hover names their contents. Slide 10 writes the lymphocyte sizes as 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 \"mm\"; they are micrometers, and he gave no numbers aloud. Slide 4 calls NK cells \"Killer T cells\" (and he said they \"function as killer T cells\"); they are drawn as the large lymphocytes, not as T cells (his slide 13: no B or T cell markers).</li><li>Mescher AL, Junqueira's Basic Histology (the blood: its leukocyte table), and Ross MH and Pawlina W, Histology: A Text and Atlas (blood): the relative sizes the cells are drawn at, 5 drawing units per micrometer (red cell about 7.5 µm, neutrophil about 12, eosinophil about 13, basophil about 11.5, monocyte about 16, small lymphocyte 7, large lymphocyte 10.5), and how each looks: neutrophil lobes joined by thin chromatin strands, the eosinophil's two lobes and large round granules, basophil granules that often hide the nucleus, the monocyte's ample cytoplasm and eccentric indented nucleus, the lymphocyte's dense nucleus. Only the lecture's numbers are labeled.</li><li>Bain BJ, Blood Cells: A Practical Guide: the colors of a stained smear the drawing translates into the hub's palette (orange-red eosinophil granules, deep purple basophil granules, gray-blue monocyte cytoplasm, pale blue lymphocyte cytoplasm, the red cell's central pallor).</li><li>Schematic, not micrographs: shapes and colors are simplified, granule counts are illustrative, and every cell is drawn flat. The NK cell is drawn without granules, because the lecture describes the lymphocytes as agranulocytes. Educational, not medical advice.</li></ul></details></div>",
